---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layouts.astro";
---

<Layout title="About">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">ABOUT ME</p>
        <h1>
            Physics, data and a growing passion for motorsport.
        </h1>
        <p class="intro">
            I'm a fourth-year Physics with Astrophysics MSc student developing my skills in data analysis, 
            programming, and vehicle dynamics, with the goal of building a career in motorsport.
            <br>
            <br>
            I created this website as a space to bring together my projects, technical articles, and learning 
            journey, while documenting how my skills and interests continue to develop.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Motorsport journey -->
    <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">MY JOURNEY</p>
        <h2>How I found motorsport</h2>
        <p>
            I actually only got into motorsport at the start of 2026. A group of content creators I enjoy 
            watching took part in a Formula E Evo session, which I found fascinating. From there, I decided 
            to start following Formula 1 and quickly fell in love with the sport.
            <br>
            <br>
            Since then, I've also enjoyed following other series, including Formula 2, Formula 3, and F1 Academy, 
            while occasionally watching WEC and IndyCar. One of the things I love most about motorsport is how much 
            there is to learn. From the cars and the physics behind them to strategy, different driving styles, and 
            the wider politics of the sport, there always seems to be another layer to explore.
            <br>
            <br>
            I started thinking about a career in motorsport almost as soon as I began following the sport, but it wasn't 
            until I finished my third year of university that I seriously started looking into what that could involve. 
            For a long time, I had convinced myself that it was unrealistic and unachievable. However, once I moved past 
            that mindset and began properly exploring the opportunities available, it was the first time in a long time 
            that I could see a clear career path for myself and genuinely felt driven to pursue it.
            <br>
            <br>
            Looking back, I don't think I realised quite how lost I had felt about where my life was heading. Having a 
            career goal that genuinely excites me has given me a new sense of direction and motivation. I'm now ready to 
            work as hard as I can to develop my skills, gain experience, and give myself the best possible chance of making 
            that goal a reality.
            <br>
            <br>
            I was lucky enough to attend the British Grand Prix at Silverstone this year - such an incredible experience! Seeing 
            the cars and experiencing the atmosphere of a race weekend in person was something I'll never forget. Walking alongside 
            the pit lane after the race was particularly inspiring, and I remember telling myself that one day, I wanted to make it 
            to the other side of the fence.
        </p>
        <section class = "figure-section">
            <figure class="medium-figure">
                <img
                    src="/images/about/silvo.jpg"
                    alt="Silverstone Collage"
                />
            </figure> 
            <figcaption>
                A few snaps from my experience at Silverstone.
            </figcaption>
        </section>
    </section>


    <!-- Goals -->
    <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">LOOKING AHEAD</p>
        <h2>My Goals</h2>
        <p>
            My long-term goal is to build a career in motorsport, ideally working in vehicle performance. I'm particularly interested 
            in vehicle dynamics and performance analysis, and I enjoy using data and physics to better understand how a car behaves and 
            where performance can be found.
            <br>
            <br>
            I'm still at the beginning of that journey, and there is a huge amount for me to learn. Over the coming years, I want to 
            continue developing my technical skills, gain hands-on experience, and explore different areas of performance engineering 
            as I work towards finding my place within the sport.
        </p>
    </section>


    <!-- Experiences -->
    <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">EXPERIENCE</p>
        <h2>Experiences That Shaped Me</h2>
        <div class="experiences">

            <!-- Work -->
            <div class="experience">
                <div class="experience-heading">
                    <p class="experience-label">WORK & RESPONSIBILITY</p>
                    <h3>My Job at Reiss</h3>
                </div>
                <div class="experience-content">
                    <p>
                        On paper, Reiss may sound like just a part-time job - somewhere to earn money and work during the weekends 
                        and over summer - but I genuinely feel that it has played a huge role in shaping me as a person.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        I started working there at 16: quiet, shy, awkward, and with very little self-confidence. Over the years, 
                        the job has gradually brought me out of my shell and made me much more comfortable interacting with people. 
                        I've gained the confidence to approach and talk to others, and I often joke that the job "made me a real 
                        person" - but there is definitely some truth in that. It taught me so many of the fundamentals that have 
                        helped me grow into the person I am today.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        One of the biggest things it taught me was to trust myself. I struggled with a lot of self-doubt when I was 
                        younger and often worried about whether I knew what I was doing. Time and experience helped me build confidence 
                        in my own abilities, while the mistakes I've made along the way taught me something equally important: there is 
                        nothing wrong with being unsure, and asking for help is almost always better than pretending you have all the 
                        answers.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        Reiss is also a very small team, and my colleagues are genuinely like family to me. With just 11 people working 
                        across the whole store, being a team player is essential. When the store gets busy, everyone has to be reliable 
                        and aware of what is happening around them. I've learned to recognise when a colleague is becoming overwhelmed 
                        and to step in where I can, while also learning that it's okay to rely on others when I need support myself.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        Working in a small team has also meant taking on a greater level of responsibility. Over the years, I've helped 
                        with end-of-day banking, locking up, deliveries, stocktake, and managing an entire floor independently when 
                        staffing levels are lower. These responsibilities have helped me develop my organisation and time-management 
                        skills, particularly when balancing customer service with keeping the shop floor organised and running smoothly.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        Perhaps most importantly, the job has taught me how to stay calm under pressure. During particularly busy periods, 
                        there is no option to become overwhelmed - you simply have to focus, take things one customer at a time, and do the 
                        best job you can. It's a skill that I know will stay with me long after my time in retail.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Trackside -->
            <div class="experience">
                <div class="experience-heading">
                    <p class="experience-label">TRACKSIDE</p>
                    <h3>Marshalling at Knockhill</h3>
                </div>
                <div class="experience-content">
                    <p>
                        Marshalling has given me the opportunity to become involved in motorsport in a completely different way. I genuinely 
                        enjoy giving my time to help events run safely, and there is something incredibly rewarding about knowing that, even 
                        in a small way, you are contributing to the safety of the drivers, teams, and everyone working at the circuit.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        I've mainly worked on flags, which has definitely developed my situational awareness. You have to remain switched on 
                        at all times, paying attention to what is happening around the circuit and being ready to react quickly when something 
                        changes. It has taught me the importance of staying focused and aware of your surroundings, particularly in an 
                        environment where situations can change very quickly.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        More than anything, though, I simply love being at the circuit and being part of the wider motorsport community. 
                        Marshalling has allowed me to contribute to a sport that I care deeply about, while gaining a much closer 
                        appreciation for everything that happens behind the scenes to keep a race weekend running safely.

                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>


            <!-- Camp America -->
            <div class="experience">
                <div class="experience-heading">
                    <p class="experience-label">INTERNATIONAL EXPERIENCE</p>
                    <h3>Camp America</h3>
                </div>
                <div class="experience-content">
                    <p>
                        Working at a summer camp was such a special experience. I met so many incredible people and formed friendships 
                        and relationships that I know I will always value.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        The camp was residential, meaning I lived in a bunk with my own group of girls alongside a few other counsellors. 
                        At first, being given that level of responsibility felt quite daunting, but I quickly grew into the role. Living and 
                        working so closely with the children meant that you were always "on", and it was certainly the ultimate test of 
                        patience, but I absolutely loved it. I became incredibly attached to my group and would have done anything for 
                        those girls.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        Being away from home for such a long period was challenging at times, but it also gave me a huge sense of 
                        independence. The experience pushed me well outside my comfort zone and showed me that I was capable of 
                        adapting to situations that initially felt unfamiliar or intimidating.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        Working as a STEM counsellor was also more challenging than I had expected. There were only two of us, and we were 
                        responsible for planning our own activities and organising the materials we needed. At first, that felt like an 
                        overwhelming amount of responsibility, but we figured it out together. Seeing the children become genuinely 
                        interested and excited about science as a result of the activities we had created made all of the work worthwhile.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        It was particularly rewarding to see how much some of the children valued having the opportunity to explore those 
                        interests. You could tell that not everyone had access to the same opportunities to engage with STEM, and being able 
                        to help nurture that curiosity felt incredibly meaningful. We were also fortunate to receive some really positive 
                        feedback, with several people telling us that it was the first time in years they had seen the children genuinely 
                        enjoy STEM activities.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Learning -->
    <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">DEVELOPMENT</p>
        <h2>My Experience at University</h2>
        <p>
            University has challenged me in ways I didn't always expect. For a long time, I struggled with motivation and a lot of self-doubt. 
            I set very high standards for myself, but at the same time, I was often convinced that I wasn't capable of reaching them. This 
            created a difficult cycle where I cared a lot about doing well but didn't always trust myself enough to believe that I could.
            <br>
            <br>
            Third year became a real turning point for me. After a particularly difficult second semester, including the loss of a family 
            member in the lead-up to my exams, I felt completely exhausted and unsure of how I was going to get through the end of the year. 
            But, I dug deep, turned things around, and achieved results that I am genuinely proud of. 
            That experience has changed how I approach challenges. I still set high standards for myself, and I still have moments of 
            self-doubt, but I have become much better at recognising that feeling unsure doesn't mean I am incapable. Sometimes the work 
            feels difficult simply because I am pushing myself to learn something new.
            <br>
            <br>
            Being a woman in STEM is also something that is important to me. I will be honest: there have been times when I have found it 
            challenging, particularly when I have felt that people were quicker to assume that I didn't know what I was doing. But I've also 
            learned to take a certain amount of satisfaction in proving those assumptions wrong. If someone underestimates me, that's okay - 
            it just gives me the opportunity to surprise them.

        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Current -->
    <section>
        <p class="eyebrow">RIGHT NOW</p>
        <h2>Currently...</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Building motorsport data analysis projects </strong> - currently working on an analysis of the Dutch Grand Prix.</li>
            <li><strong>Developing my MATLAB and Simulink skills </strong> - completing onramps and courses on MathWorks</li>
            <li><strong>Learning more about vehicle dynamics </strong> - reading Race Car Vehicle Dynamics by Milliken and Milliken.</li>
            <li><strong>Getting involved with Formula Student </strong> - preparing to join UGRacing in September.</li>
            <li><strong>Writing technical and race analysis </strong> - I would like my next piece to be a preview for Monza, with as much 
                info on upgrades as I can find. </li>
        </ul>
    </section>


    <!-- CV -->
    <section class="cv-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">MORE INFORMATION</p>
        <h2>Want the formal version?</h2>
        <p>
            You can find a more detailed overview of my education, my CV and my LinkedIn below.
        </p>
        <div class="buttons">
            <a href="/cv.pdf" class="button">
                VIEW CV
            </a>
            <a href="/unitranscript.pdf" class="button">
                VIEW ACADEMIC REPORT
            </a>            
            <a
                href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/oliviag-mcfadden73249b380/"
                class="button secondary"
                target="_blank"
            >
                LINKEDIN ↗
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>
</Layout>


<style>
    .hero {
        padding: 0.7rem 0 1rem;
    }

    section .eyebrow {
        color: var(--accent);
        font-size: 0.8rem;
        letter-spacing: 0.15em;
        font-weight: 600;
        margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }

    h1 {
        font-size: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 6rem);
        line-height: 0.95;
        margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    }


    .intro {
        font-size: 1.25rem;
        line-height: 1.7;
        color: var(--secondary);
    }

    .figure-section img {
        display: block;
        width: 100%;
        height: auto;
    }

    .figure-section figure {
        margin: 0;
    }    

    .medium-figure {
        max-width: 100%;
    }

    figcaption {
        color: var(--secondary);
        font-size: 0.85rem;
        line-height: 1.5;
        margin-top: 1rem;
    }
    
    .figure-section {
        padding: 1rem 0;
    }    

    .bold {
        font-weight: bold;
        color: black;
    }
    /* GENERAL SECTIONS */

    section:not(.hero) {
        padding: 1rem 0;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    }


    h2 {
        font-size: 2rem;
        margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }


    section p {
        line-height: 1.6;
        color: var(--secondary);
    }


    /* EXPERIENCES */
   .experiences {
        margin-top: 1rem;
    }

    .experience {
        padding: 1rem 0;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    }

    .experience-heading {
        align-self: start;
    }

    section .experience-label {
        font-size: 0.75rem;
        letter-spacing: 0.15em;
        font-weight: 600;
        color: var(--accent);
        margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    }

    .experience h3 {
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 1.4rem;
    }

    .experience-content p {
        margin-top: 0.7rem;
        margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }


    /* CURRENTLY */

    ul {
        padding-left: 1.2rem;
        line-height: 2;
        color: var(--secondary);
    }


    /* CV */

    .cv-section {
        text-align: center;
    }


    .cv-section p {
        margin-left: auto;
        margin-right: auto;
    }


    .buttons {
        display: flex;
        justify-content: center;
        gap: 1rem;
        margin-top: 2rem;
    }


    .button {
        display: inline-block;
        padding: 0.9rem 1.4rem;
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        text-decoration: none;
        font-size: 0.85rem;
        letter-spacing: 0.1em;
        color: var(--primary);
    }


    .button:hover {
        border-color: var(--accent);
        color: var(--accent);
    }


    /* MOBILE */

    @media (max-width: 700px) {
        .experience {
            grid-template-columns: 1fr;
            gap: 1.5rem;
        }


        .hero {
            padding: 4rem 0 5rem;
        }


        section:not(.hero) {
            padding: 4rem 0;
        }


        .buttons {
            flex-direction: column;
            align-items: center;
        }

    }

</style>